# Module: telemetry
Compile unit for the Orchestration Service.
Generates: `orchestrator/telemetry.py`

## Role
The orchestrator's **Redis pub/sub gateway**, plus the write-only `decision_log` (Postgres) governance record. Three channels, one module: the permanent `decision_log`, the public `agent_events` ticker, and the private per-author `screening_results` notice. Every phase module (`ingestion_worker`, `pm_agent`, `architect`, `compiler_writer`) imports this for all three — no phase module publishes to Redis directly, and no phase module constructs its own Redis client.
This is **not** the seam for a phase module's own Postgres row work (`pm_agent` inserting `feature_requests`/`feature_votes`, `architect`/`lifecycle` flipping `status`, etc.) — those modules hold their own injected Supabase client for that, same convention as the backend's `deps.py`. `telemetry` only owns the three channels above, `decision_log` being the one Postgres table among them (grouped in here because, like the other two, every write is a governance/observability record fired at the moment a decision is made, never touched again).

## Clients (injection seam)
The **only** place in the orchestrator that holds a Redis client (for all three channels above). Whichever process starts first (`ingestion_service` or `sprint_service`) constructs it once at startup and passes it into `telemetry` — this module never builds its own connection. It also holds the Supabase client it needs for its own `decision_log` inserts — the same client instance is injected here and into every phase module that needs Postgres for its primary work, all constructed once at process startup, mirroring the backend's `deps.py` convention. Unit tests inject `fakeredis` and a stubbed Supabase client here.

## Interface
- `async log_decision(feature_id, batch_id, phase, agent, decision: dict, model_version)` — insert into `decision_log`.
  Phases: `screening | dedup | friction | compile | deploy | lifecycle`.
- `async publish_agent_event(phase, agent_name, message)` — `PUBLISH` to `AGENT_EVENTS_CHANNEL` with the `AgentEventPayload` shape from `contracts.py` (`{phase, agent_name, message}`). This is **the only path** to the ticker — the backend's `event_relay` only ever hears about a phase's progress through this call.
  Phases (ticker-facing, a narrower set than `log_decision`'s): `screening | synthesizing | architecting | compiling | deployed` — every phase module publishes at least one of these per run (`ingestion_worker` → `screening`, `pm_agent` → `synthesizing`, `architect` → `architecting` in both its modes, `compiler_writer` → `compiling`); `deployed` is emitted directly by the backend's `routes/webhook.py` instead (it already owns the deploy confirmation moment synchronously, so it writes `broadcast_events` straight rather than round-tripping through Redis).
- `async publish_screening_result(feature_id, author_id, reason, shipped_version=None, merged_into_feature_id=None, merged_into_title=None)` — `PUBLISH` to `SCREENING_RESULTS_CHANNEL` with the payload shape from `contracts.py`. Called by `ingestion_worker` (`security`/`off_topic`/`unclear` drops) and `pm_agent` (`already_shipped`, `merged`). Private per-author channel — the backend's `event_relay` consumes it to update `pending_pitch:*`, and it is **never** relayed to `broadcast_events`.

## Behavior
- `log_decision` is insert-only against `decision_log`; never UPDATE or DELETE (the table is the permanent evaluator-training dataset).
- **Content-free rule for dropped features**: screening entries for MALICIOUS/OFF_TOPIC/UNCLEAR drops carry `feature_id`, classification, reason category, and model version — never the dropped title or description text. Survivors may be referenced by `feature_id` (their content is persisted normally in Phase 2).
- `publish_agent_event` sends aggregate/narrative copy only — a phase module must never pass pitch title/description text through as `message`; that boundary is the caller's responsibility (this module doesn't inspect content). `publish_screening_result` carries only ids/reason/denormalized titles, per the same content-free rule — never the pitch's own title or description.
- No call ever blocks the pipeline on failure: log a warning and continue, but count failures for ops visibility. A dropped ticker or screening-result event degrades to a quieter UI, never a stalled phase; a dropped `decision_log` write degrades the audit trail, never the decision itself.

